<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="use-a-published-pack"
      updated="August 18, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"choose", "Choose a pack"},
        {"inspect", "See what you are adding"},
        {"install", "Install a fixed version"},
        {"configure", "Configure credentials"},
        {"verify", "Verify the pack"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="use-a-published-pack">
        <:dek>
          Add a published pack to one runner: review its actions first, install a fixed
          version, give it credentials, and verify one action.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_h2 id="choose">1. Choose a pack</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        On the host, list the published packs that match it. The command checks what the host
        runs — programs, processes, ports — and installs nothing.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">sudo emisar pack suggest</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        You can also browse the <.link
          navigate={~p"/packs"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >
          public pack catalog
        </.link>. Pick the pack for the service this runner hosts.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="inspect">2. See what you are adding</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The pack's catalog page lists every action your agent will gain — with its arguments,
        side effects, and risk tier — plus the host requirements. Skim the list to know what the
        pack does; under the default policy, the risky actions wait for a person no matter what
        you install. Write down the pack ID, the version, and the full <code>sha256</code>
        hash — the install pins all three.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="install">3. Install a fixed version</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Pin what you reviewed: <code>&lt;pack&gt;=&lt;version&gt;</code>
        picks the published version, and <code>--hash</code>
        makes the runner reject anything but those exact bytes.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">sudo emisar pack install redis=0.2.3 \
  --hash sha256:&lt;full-reviewed-hash&gt;</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The command validates the pack, reloads a running runner, and ends by probing the
        pack's declared verify action — a wrong or missing credential shows up now, at the
        terminal, and the next step fixes it. A public catalog hash becomes
        trusted automatically. A custom hash stays pending on <a
          href={~p"/app/packs"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Packs</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Installing from a private <code>packctl</code>
        registry uses its immutable URLs — see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/pack-registry"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Host your own registry</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="configure">4. Configure credentials</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each pack ships its own setup guide — which credentials it needs and how to create
        them (the github-cli pack, for example, links the exact token page with the right scopes
        preselected). It appears on the pack's catalog page under <strong class="text-zinc-200">Setup</strong>, and on the host:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">sudo emisar pack info redis</.docs_code>
      <ol class="mt-5 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base text-zinc-400">
        <li>Install every missing program named by the pack.</li>
        <li>Put each credential value in <code>/etc/emisar/runner.env</code>.</li>
        <li>
          Add each required variable name to <code>execution.inherit_env</code>
          in <code>/etc/emisar/config.yaml</code>.
        </li>
        <li>Restart the service with <code>sudo systemctl restart emisar</code>.</li>
        <li>
          Run <code>sudo emisar pack verify redis</code> — the probe that failed before the
          credentials were in place passes now.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Keep credential values out of action arguments and pack YAML. See
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet" <> "#pack-credentials"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Pack credentials</.link>
        for the complete environment pattern.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="verify">5. Verify the pack</.docs_h2>
      <ol class="mt-5 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          Run <code>sudo emisar doctor --probe</code>. It checks the host and runs every
          pack's verify action. Fix every pack error.
        </li>
        <li>
          Open <a
            href={~p"/app/runners"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Runners</a>. Confirm that the runner advertises the new actions.
        </li>
        <li>
          Open <a
            href={~p"/app/packs"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Packs</a>. Confirm the exact version, hash, and trust state.
        </li>
        <li>Run one low-risk read action from the pack.</li>
        <li>
          Open <a
            href={~p"/app/audit"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Audit</a>. Confirm that the event records the pack, action, runner, and operator.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        For a fleet change, use a canary and bounded batches. See <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/pack-updates"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Roll out and roll back packs</.link>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A pack that never appears, or an action missing from a runner that should have it —
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/pack-updates" <> "#troubleshooting"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Troubleshooting</.link>
        separates the causes.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
